[% header('Albanian stemming algorithm') %]

<h2>Links to resources</h2>

<ul>
[% algorithm_lis('albanian', 'Albanian', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albanian_language') %]
</ul>

<p>
The algorithm was developed in a
<a href="https://github.com/edisnord/albanian-stemmer">separate repository</a>,
which generates the Snowball source from a rules file and keeps the data and
measurements behind each choice described here.
</p>

<h2>Design Notes</h2>

<p>
This is a light, inflectional stemmer for keyword search in standard
(Tosk-based) Albanian.  It removes the endings that inflect a word for case,
definiteness, number, person, tense and mood, and conflates derived words in
only two places (below), on purpose.
</p>

<p>
The candidate endings come from the Academy grammar (<i>Gramatika e gjuhës
shqipe 1: Morfologjia</i>, Akademia e Shkencave, Tiranë 1995).  They were then
pruned against lexicons of Albanian text tagged with lemmas: an ending was
kept only while it made words sharing a lemma share a stem more often, without
letting precision (words with the same stem sharing a lemma) fall below a
fixed floor.  The result was checked on BM25 retrieval benchmarks and on a
sample of 300 word families labelled by hand.
</p>

<h3>Folding ë and ç</h3>

<p>
The stemmer first replaces <i>ë</i> with <i>e</i> and <i>ç</i> with <i>c</i>.
Much Albanian text on the web, and more of what people type into a search
box, is written without them (<i>shume</i> for <i>shumë</i>, <i>cfare</i>
for <i>çfarë</i>): in a sample of web text 4.5% of the occurrences of words
spelt with ë or ç were written without them, and 12.7% in a crawl of public
documents.  Keeping ë in the stems separates more homographs (<i>rend</i>
"order" and <i>rëndë</i> "heavy") but lost far more retrieval quality than it
gained, so stems are always written without the diacritics.
</p>

<h3>The definite article comes first</h3>

<p>
As in Romanian, Albanian suffixes its definite article and fuses it with the
case ending (<i>mollë</i>, <i>molla</i>, <i>mollës</i>, <i>mollën</i>,
<i>mollët</i>, <i>mollëve</i> "apple"), and verbs stack person and tense on
the stem (<i>punoj</i>, <i>punonte</i>, <i>punohet</i>, <i>punuar</i>
"work").  So, as in the <a href="/algorithms/romanian/stemmer.html">Romanian
stemmer</a>, the longest article, case or verb ending is removed first
(step 1), then a plural or stem vowel in front of it (<i>-e</i>, <i>-a</i>,
<i>-enj</i>, <i>-inj</i>; step 2).  All of these become <i>moll</i> and
<i>pun</i>.
</p>

<p>
Step 1 holds the <i>-oj</i> conjugation written out whole (<i>-onte</i>,
<i>-ohet</i>, <i>-uar</i>, <i>-uara</i>, <i>-uarit</i>, <i>-ua</i>), not the
thematic <i>o</i>/<i>ua</i> on its own: removing a bare <i>-o</i> or
<i>-u</i> would also cut nouns such as <i>radio</i> and <i>kilo</i>, and
merge <i>tregues</i> "indicator" with <i>treg</i> "market".
</p>

<h3>Minimum stem and the letter before an ending</h3>

<p>
No ending is removed from the first three letters, so a stem keeps at least
three.  Letting stems go down to two letters merged too many unrelated short
words.
</p>

<p>
Several short endings are also verb or noun stems, so, as in the Finnish and
Turkish stemmers, they come off only after a particular kind of letter: a
vowel (<i>-t</i>, <i>-s</i>, <i>-j</i>, <i>-ja</i>, <i>-ra</i>, <i>-het</i>
and others: <i>mollët</i> loses <i>-t</i> after <i>ë</i>, but <i>rast</i>
"case" keeps its <i>t</i>), or <i>k</i>, <i>g</i> or <i>h</i> (<i>-am</i>,
<i>-rin</i>, <i>-rit</i>).  The admirative <i>-ka</i> (<i>hapka</i>) is not
an ending at all: in <i>banka</i>, <i>maska</i> and <i>marka</i> the <i>k</i>
is the root's, and those nouns are far more frequent.  Which ending takes which
condition was chosen against the lexicons; the conditions raised recall
without costing precision.  The usual Snowball regions were tried for the same job
and did less well, so they are used only for <i>-im</i> (below).
</p>

<p>
Some endings test the letters themselves, where a word's own letters look
like the ending.  Each condition was read off a lexicon, by counting for every
word with the ending whether its lemma is the word itself or another word:
</p>

<ul>
<li><i>-em</i> not after <i>iz</i>, <i>bl</i> or <i>st</i>: <i>hapem</i>,
    <i>falem</i> lose it, <i>organizëm</i>, <i>problem</i>, <i>sistem</i> keep it;</li>
<li><i>-së</i> after a vowel but not after <i>je</i>: <i>shtëpisë</i> loses
    it, <i>pjesë</i> keeps it;</li>
<li><i>-nim</i> after <i>o</i> or <i>a</i> but not <i>io</i>: the imperfect
    <i>punonim</i> loses it, the nouns <i>bashkëpunim</i>, <i>funksionim</i>
    keep their <i>n</i>;</li>
<li><i>-nte</i> not after <i>e</i> (<i>punonte</i>, not <i>dokumente</i>),
    <i>-ve</i> not after <i>o</i> or <i>ti</i> (<i>punëve</i>, not
    <i>Kosovë</i>, <i>sportive</i>), <i>-ra</i> only after <i>ë</i>
    (<i>gjëra</i>, not <i>mënyra</i>), <i>-hem</i> not after <i>s</i>
    (<i>punohem</i>, not <i>rëndësishëm</i>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tidying the stem</h3>

<p>
After the endings, the end of the stem is normalized so that the forms of a
paradigm meet: a final <i>e</i> or <i>j</i> goes (<i>sëmundje</i>,
<i>sëmundjet</i> &rarr; <i>semund</i>), <i>k</i> becomes <i>q</i> (<i>mik</i>,
<i>miqtë</i> &rarr; <i>miq</i>), <i>er</i> becomes <i>r</i> (<i>libër</i>,
<i>librin</i> &rarr; <i>libr</i>), and <i>odh</i> becomes <i>edh</i>
(<i>zgjedh</i>, <i>zgjodhi</i> "choose", "chose" &rarr; <i>zgjedh</i>).  A few
noun classes change their stem too: <i>ull</i> becomes <i>u</i>
(<i>artikull</i>, plural <i>artikuj</i> &rarr; <i>artiku</i>), <i>izem</i>
becomes <i>izm</i> (<i>organizëm</i>, <i>organizmi</i>), <i>itet</i> and
<i>ytet</i> lose their <i>et</i> (<i>aktivitet</i>, <i>aktiviteti</i> &rarr;
<i>aktivit</i>; <i>qytet</i>, <i>qyteti</i> &rarr; <i>qyt</i>), and
<i>shm</i> becomes <i>sh</i> (<i>rëndësishëm</i>, <i>rëndësishme</i>), but
not after a vowel other than <i>i</i> (<i>dëshmi</i> keeps its
<i>m</i>).  The rewrites apply to every stem ending that way, so a stem
need not be a word (<i>ndodh</i> &rarr; <i>ndedh</i>); what matters is that a
paradigm's forms meet.
</p>

<h3>Two derivations, on purpose</h3>

<p>
Derived words otherwise keep their own stems (<i>punëtor</i> "worker" stays
apart from <i>punoj</i> "work"), but two derivations are conflated because
searchers expect them to be:
</p>

<ul>
<li>an <i>-oj</i> verb meets the noun it is made from, since the verb ending
    goes whole (<i>kontrolloj</i>, <i>kontrollit</i> &rarr; <i>kontroll</i>);</li>
<li>a noun of action in <i>-im</i> meets its verb: a third step removes
    <i>-im</i> once step 1 has taken the article (<i>përdorimi</i>,
    <i>përdor</i> &rarr; <i>perdor</i>).  It applies only in R2, so short
    words keep it (<i>trim</i>, <i>klim</i>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Exceptions</h3>

<p>
A table of whole words is looked up before any ending is removed, as in the
English (Porter2) stemmer.  It holds two kinds of word:
</p>

<ul>
<li>the forms of the irregular verbs of the grammar's second and third
    groups (48 verbs), which share no stem a suffix rule could reach
    (<i>vij</i>, <i>vjen</i>, <i>erdhi</i>, <i>ardhur</i> &rarr; <i>erdh</i>;
    <i>jam</i>, <i>është</i>, <i>ishte</i>, <i>qenë</i> &rarr; <i>je</i>),
    each verb mapped to a stem no other word has;</li>
<li>the plurals of the 17 nouns whose plural is a different stem
    (<i>dorë</i>, <i>duar</i> "hand"; <i>grua</i>, <i>gra</i> "woman";
    <i>derë</i>, <i>dyer</i> "door"), mapped to the singular's stem;</li>
<li><i>shtet</i> "state": the bare form would lose <i>-et</i>, like a plural,
    and the stem left, <i>sht</i>, is that of <i>shtoj</i> "add", so no
    letter rule can tell them apart.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The verb forms are every form of the verbs' conjugation tables, as given by
the online dictionary fjalorthi.com, and those found in the corpus.  A form
that is also another word stays with the rules (<i>parë</i> "seen" or
"first", <i>tha</i> "said" or "dried"; <i>vinte</i> of both <i>vij</i>
"come" and <i>vë</i> "put").  The table holds no list of other words: what
an ending would split is handled by the letter conditions above.  The auxiliaries
<i>jam</i> "be" and <i>kam</i> "have" are listed although most of their
forms are also in the stop list, so that the verb gets one stem when no stop
list is applied.  Removing stopwords made no measurable difference to BM25
retrieval.
</p>

<h3>What it does not do</h3>

<ul>
<li>It does not remove prefixes.</li>
<li>Homographs that only ë tells apart are merged (<i>para</i> "money" and
    <i>parë</i> "first"; <i>rend</i> and <i>rëndë</i>).</li>
<li>Gheg forms are only partly covered (the participle in <i>-ue</i> is).</li>
</ul>

